body:
## Problem

When the game starts in fullscreen mode and the user presses Windows key + D (show desktop), the game displays with an "extreme zoomed in" view due to incorrect viewport dimensions being applied during the fullscreen state transition.

## Root Cause

In `RenderWindowD3D11::Update()`, when the fullscreen state changes due to external events like Windows key + D, the code was using stale `m_pendingWidth`/`m_pendingHeight` values or falling back to the current `m_width`/`m_height` without querying the actual current window dimensions. This resulted in the viewport being resized to incorrect dimensions, causing the zoom effect.

## Solution

Modified the fullscreen state change handling to call `GetClientRect()` to get the actual current window client area dimensions when the DirectX fullscreen state changes. The fix includes:

- **Accurate dimension retrieval**: Uses `GetClientRect()` to get real-time window dimensions
- **Proper validation**: Ensures dimensions are positive before using them
- **Robust error handling**: Falls back to current dimensions if the API call fails
- **Consistent patterns**: Follows existing code style used elsewhere in the same file

## Code Changes

```cpp
// Before: Used stale or current dimensions
if (m_pendingWidth == 0 || m_pendingHeight == 0)
{
    m_pendingWidth = m_width;
    m_pendingHeight = m_height;
}

// After: Get actual current window dimensions
RECT clientRect;
if (GetClientRect(m_handle, &clientRect))
{
    const uint16 actualWidth = static_cast<uint16>(clientRect.right - clientRect.left);
    const uint16 actualHeight = static_cast<uint16>(clientRect.bottom - clientRect.top);

    if (actualWidth > 0 && actualHeight > 0)
    {
        m_pendingWidth = actualWidth;
        m_pendingHeight = actualHeight;
    }
    // ... fallback logic
}
```

## Testing

- Verified logic handles success, zero dimensions, and API failure scenarios correctly
- Follows existing patterns used in the same file for window dimension queries
- No breaking changes to existing functionality

Fixes #103.

